  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nThe surface high pressure that has been our primary weather driver\nwill continue to push east on Tuesday. This allows return flow to\npercolate through the region and mid level ridging to move over the\nCWA. This combination will result in an influx of higher temperatures\na dew points Tuesday into Wednesday. Heat indices will once again\nrise into the 90s, with Wednesday being the hotter of the two days.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2) \n\nA short wave will track from the northern Plains across the upper\nGreat Lakes, pulling a cold front into the area Wednesday night.\nMoisture out ahead of the front appears to be limited, but some \ninstability does develop, with around 2000 J/kg of SBCAPE being \nrealized by Wednesday afternoon. The strongest bulk shear remains off\nto our north, possibly tickling areas north of I-71. Should \nthunderstorms fire off during the late afternoon into evening hours, \nsome strong to severe storms would be possible, especially for the\nnorthwestern portion of our CWA, where deeper shear may aid in storm\norganization. \n\nAs the front slowly sags down, it looks to take on a southwest to\nnortheast orientation and be very slow to progress. PWATs along the \nfront are forecast to be around 200% of normal. As any robust CAPE \ngets pinched off and a a southwesterly overnight LLJ ramps up, the \nthreat for repeated rounds of storms (training) increases. This\nintroduces a heavy rainfall and flooding threat during the overnight\nhours Wednesday into Thursday.\n\nBy Thursday, the front will still be laid out across the Ohio \nValley, nearly stalled out. This continues the chances for repeated \nrounds of showers Thursday morning before more robust convection \nredevelops Thursday afternoon with diurnal heating and the increase \nin mid level forcing. This will bring a renewed threat of heavy rain \nand training cells, primarily across the southern counties. \n\nAll of this activity will push southeast of the area, along with the\nfront, on Thursday evening. Cooler and drier conditions will return\nfor the end of the week.",
